# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 1.0753e+03 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 2.2500 79.120 4.0809e+04 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~79 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 4.4580 58.675 1.3016e+06 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.07 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 - drag_overtakes_ablation 4.4602 58.647 1.3060e+06 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
5 F flare 4.4818 58.456 3.0406e+06 stylized  Light rises x4.7 in 0.13 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 58.6 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 58.2 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 34% of the rise and 27% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
6 B first_breakup 4.5025 58.265 5.6634e+06 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.4 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
7 F flare 4.8458 55.125 1.0149e+07 stylized  Light rises x1.9 in 0.11 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 55.3 km (37% of m0); split gen 1 at 54.9 km (37% of m0). Dust groups supply 13% of the rise and 20% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 b bump 5.1545 52.373 1.7052e+07 stylized  Small fluctuation, 6% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 1) at 52.4 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
9 b bump 5.3296 50.859 1.8216e+07 stylized  Small fluctuation, 9% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 1) at 50.9 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
10 P peak 5.4387 49.923 1.9593e+07 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.7 km from the peak of energy deposition (49.2 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 12.5 km/s).
11 b bump 5.4494 49.847 1.8579e+07 stylized  Small fluctuation, 11% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 1) at 49.8 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
12 D rolloff 6.2005 44.287 7.2075e+06 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.76 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 70% of v0, 62% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
13 T tail 8.6485 31.634 1.9366e+04 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
